  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1031 AM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft over the Southern Plains early this week will \nkeep storm coverage limited even though moisture levels remain above \nnormal. PWs around 1.2\" are modeled, which would allow for higher \nrain rates and isolated flash flooding. However, the warmer air \naloft from the ridge will lower the convective potential each day. \nThrough midweek, synoptic models are not showing any noticeable \nfeatures such as shortwaves/vort maxes that provide additional \nforcing to the typical orographic lift. We'll have sufficient \nmoisture and instability each afternoon for convection to develop, \nbut are lacking a trigger for much of the week. \n\nFor today, the better moisture will be focused out west (as shown\nby current WV imagery) with drier conditions along and E of the \nRGV. There is a marginal risk of flash flooding W of the Divide \nlater today with rainfall over 1\" possible in spots. Spotty \nshowers and storms are forecast for the rest of the lowlands with \nconvection favored over the west slopes of the Sacs this \nafternoon. Outflow gusts to 30 mph are possible today.\n\nThe ridge axis remains extended through NM for Mon with slightly \nlower rain chances area-wide, still favoring out west underneath the \nmoisture plume. Mainly dry conditions are forecast for the lowlands \non Mon. The high elongates across the southern US into midweek due \nto a passing upper trough across the Midwest. Again, forcing will be \nhard to come by due to the warmer air aloft and general subsidence. \nLimited storm chances through Thu with better coverage out west. The \nupper high re-establishes over the Desert SW late in the week as \nmoisture is recycled from the north around it. This may be the \nbeginning of the end of the monsoon as the westerlies dig further \nsouth from the Rockies. The trough that squashes the high around \nmidweek may slip in from the N on Thu and bring some moisture and a \nweak cold front, depending on where the upper high sets up. \n\nA more significant feature to watch for is a deep upper trough \ndiving down the Rockies about a week from now, which the GEFS is \nmuch more bullish on compared to the Euro ensemble. If the GEFS is \nright, below-normal temps and breezy E/N winds would reach the \nBorderland early next week. A wave of moisture and showers/storms \ncould also accompany this front from the Plains.\n\nTemperatures will be near or slightly above normal (about 5 \ndegrees) through the week with lowland highs in the mid-upper 90s.\nEl Paso may approach 100F late in the week as the upper high\nconsolidates to the west.",
